pervised. |\n| R27 | **Bash wrapper `set -e` / pipefail / error suppression gaps** — some reviews claim missing `set -e`, suppressed `mkdir 2>/dev/null`, unchecked `cat`, `python3` rc; `state[\"agents\"]` etc. Pn6g finds `set -eu + pipefail` present (222-223), state-dir fatal, events fallback to stderr, cycle failures `|| rc` + consecutive-error cap. | #469-1/3/10, #466 set-e check | **MINOR** | observer.sh:167/222-223 `set -eu` + `pipefail`, 300 mkdir, 459 events fallback, 3172-3182 cap | scripts/observer/observer.sh:bash wrapper | **DO NOT CHANGE** — fix-pass closed the substantive gaps; remaining nits are hygiene. Keep current `set -eu` + explicit `|| rc` pattern. |\n| R28 | **Hygiene / low-blast-radius minors (grouped, each DO NOT CHANGE or DEFER)** — dead `death-classification-deferred` else (#467 M3), unused `timeout_ok` (#467 M4 / #469-4), unused `cycle_start` (#467 M5), log-line ordering PARKED vs CONSENT (#467 M6), GNU `\\|` in tests (#467 M9 — Linux CI only), KICKOFF 40k limit (#467 M10 — estimate near top), redundant `cat-file --` (#467 M11), wave_admission heavy every cycle (#465 F14), commit_age None→fresh (#469-14), doc-universe 800-file scan every cycle (#469-15), OOM vs KILLED ambiguity (#466 F19), doctrine miss deletions+stale cache (#466 F18), findings self-satisfy (#466 F17), fast-path conditional on prior probe (#466 F16), lexicographic prune sort (#469-17), row-count `count(b\"\\n\")` fragile (#465 F17), sentinel no structure validation (#469-13), python stderr not to events (#465 F11), staging heavy, etc. | #467 M3-M6,M9-M11; #469-14/15/17/13; #465 F11/F14/F17; #466 F16-F19 | **MINOR** | observer.sh:1291-1293, 436, 2442, 1613-1630, tests/run-tests.sh:91/107/138/141/160, observer.sh:2223, 929, 2290-2313, 678-688, 1431-1447, etc. | scripts/observer/observer.sh + tests | **DO NOT CHANGE** — individually negligible; batch as hygiene backlog. Fix only if touching adjacent code (e.g., anchor tests to GNU grep with `grep -E`). |\n| R29 | **CYCLE_PROG single-quoted heredoc + `python3 -c \"$CYCLE_PROG\"` quoting risk** — heredoc `<<'PY'` contains single quotes; future content change could break nesting. | #469-9 | **MAJOR** | observer.sh:2475 `python3 -c \"$CYCLE_PROG\"`, 330 `CYCLE_PROG='...'` heredoc | scripts/observer/observer.sh:bash wrapper | **DEFER** — works today (single quotes inside `'` heredoc safe, passed as one quoted arg); migrate to `python3 - <<'PY'` stdin heredoc or temp-file exec when wrapper reworked. |\n| R30 | **Agent-identity model assumes liveness scanner emits worktree slug as `agent`** — probes at WORKTREES_ROOT/agent/... (648,669,732,942) silently mis-resolve if scanner emits session IDs/tmux names → working_issue None → comments to FLAG_ISSUE. Out-of-scope (agent-liveness.py not reviewed) but contract gap. | #465 F18 (assumption) | **MINOR** | observer.sh:648,669,732,942 `WORKTREES_ROOT/agent` probes | scripts/observer/observer.sh + agent-liveness.py contract | **DEFER** — add explicit contract test (scanner ↔ observer) before multi-agent scale; not blocking single-wave shakedown. |\n| R31 | **Test non-hermeticity (T16 live-issue) + fragile output coupling** — suite red/green depends on CWD + issue #460 locked state; 96/98 from /tmp vs 181/181 in-repo. | #465 F15, #466 F20, #467 M1/M2 | **MINOR** | run-tests.sh:483-488 T16 `crosslink issue show 460` live, 96/98 vs 181/181 | scripts/observer/tests/run-tests.sh:T16 | **DO NOT CHANGE** — known limitation, hermetic harness would need fixtures/mocks; acceptable for shakedown (document CWD requirement). |\n\nDisposition tally: MUST now 4 (R01 breaker, R02 phase-before-mutation, R03 single-cycle kill, R10 CWD), SHOULD now 7 (R11 slug anchor, R12 /tmp perms, R13 schema error, R14 rclone streamed hash, R15 base-branch, R16 retry arithmetic, R17 background backup; R21 DRY_RUN gate grouped), DO NOT CHANGE 9 (R04 parked fix, R05 backup integrity CLOSED, R07 lock, R08 R2 coverage, R22 audit-only, R23 DRY_RUN=0, R27 set-e, R28 hygiene, R31 T16), DEFER 11 (R06 crash idempotency, R19 fragile CLI, R20 admission prose, R24 catalog, R25 KEEP/partial, R26 growth, R29 CYCLE_PROG quoting, R30 identity contract, plus residual export-remote R05b).\n\nAll MUST now items are bounded one-line to small-function fixes — no architecture rewrite required before unsupervised.\n\n---\n§2 — Observer design implications: F23 blocking, F24 ephemerality, F4 breaker → finite-state / explicit-contract principles\n\nThe lifecycle-manager design (`.design/lifecycle-manager-design.md`) defines the Observer as a finite-state supervisor: every agent has an explicit state in `manager-state.json` (the lifecycle-manager's statechart owns the lifecycle, the engine only schedules — \"reversed composition\" tension noted in execution-engine-ui synthesis), and every transition emits evidence via an explicit contract (compose_transition_evidence → sha256 manifest → staging → digest comment). The knowledge page `data-retention` and the EDASES methodology reserve state ownership to the statechart; the Observer must not introduce implementation assumptions that weaken the higher layers. Three findings violate these principles:\n\n**F23 — Backup pass blocks the single-threaded observation loop (5-10m blind)**\nPrinciple violated: *finite-state liveness + explicit scheduling contract*. The Observer is the sole holder of kill/cleanup authority between cycles; its contract with agents is \"observe every interval (OBSERVER_INTERVAL ~120s) → classify → act.\" Wave_backup runs synchronously inside main() after agents/fast-path/wave checks and before save (3028-3134). On live-verified multi-GB stores (2.6+2.9 GB) the hot `sqlite3 .backup` + `integrity_check` + sha256 + gzip export + read-back + optional rclone blocks the loop for 5-10m per store, delaying all monitoring including kill responses. The statechart still believes the Observer is live, but the engine has starved its own scheduler. This is a classic *lifecycle-ownership* tension: the engine claims state that the statechart reserves (the Observer owns \"when to act,\" the statechart owns \"what state means\").\nDesign implication: F23 forces a *reversed-composition correction* — the statechart's liveness guarantee must not depend on the engine's synchronous backup. Fix is to make backup a background job with an explicit contract: snapshot synchronously (fast, atomic `sqlite3 .backup` to temp), then hand off export/verify/rclone to a thread/child process that posts completion events back to the state stream (e.g., `backup_pass_completed` event). The main loop keeps scanning agents every interval; a frozen agent detected seconds before backup still gets killed same cycle (or next interval), not after 10m. This preserves the finite-state invariant: every cycle advances the lifecycle, no cycle is silently swallowed by a lower-layer concern. Cost is low (one background thread, snapshot file as handoff), value is high (F23 → SHOULD now). Unbounded, backup remains interval-gated (24h), so frequency is low, but first-run (last_run_ts=0) and retry windows are exactly when the system is most vulnerable — making the fix gating for unsupervised.\n\n**F24 — Ephemeral evidence + unbounded logs + no tamper anchoring (MAJOR per operator)**\nPrinciple violated: *durable store + provenance + cheapest-test-first*. The design promises \"evidence takes seconds, not archaeology\" (F1 header 62-69) and the statechart owns the lifecycle record; methodology derives from research, implementation derives from methodology — evidence IS methodology, not an artefact. Today all Observer memory lives under `/tmp/opencode/observer-state/` (242,297-301) and `~/opencode-archive/` append-only logs with no rotation; a reboot destroys events.jsonl, manager-state.json, evidence bundles, preserved-worktrees.txt, and the lock. Hashes are self-referential (computed by the same writer, lost with the file). This is the *cheapest-test-first* failure: the cheapest discriminating test for \"did the supervisor act correctly?\" is \"re-read the state and evidence\" — but the test is made expensive (re-archaeology) or impossible (reboot) by ephemerality. The durable-store principle from the workflow-topology design requires the hub (crosslink issues.db) plus a durable Observer ledger; today only the hub is durable, the ledger is not.\nDesign implication: the finite-state contract must be *anchored outside the writer*. Concretely: (1) MUST now — move `manager-state.json` + `events.jsonl` off `/tmp` to a durable, per-wave or per-worktree path (e.g., `~/.opencode/observer-state/` or `$REPO_ROOT/.crosslink/observer/` or under `/tmp` with hot-backup to `~/opencode-archive/manager/` each save — cheapest MUST fix is \"hot-backup the state file on every save\" analogous to the DB hot-backup). This makes the lifecycle record survive reboot and satisfies the reversed-composition rule (statechart owns durable state, engine is stateless scheduler). (2) SHOULD now — bound all append-only files (events, verification.log, sync-pending, prune-report, evidence bundles) with rotation/caps (size or count, e.g., keep N generations, truncate with index). This prevents the \"indefinite duplicates → unbounded growth → disk fill\" chain that R06+R26 describe. (3) DEFER to v2 — tamper anchoring: external signature/WORM. Self-referential sha256 is checkable only if you trust the writer; true provenance needs an external anchor (e.g., hub comment digest, sigstore, or append-only WORM mount). For supervised shakedown (reboots rare, single-operator /tmp) the current ephemerality is acceptable (reviewers: MINOR for shakedown, MAJOR for production), which is why F24 is MUST for unsupervised durability but only SHOULD/DEFER for the log-bounding and anchoring layers.\n\n**F4 (R01) — Circuit-breaker starvation of kill authority**\nPrinciple violated: *explicit-contract safety budget + cheapest-test-first for kill authority*. The breaker is the explicit contract that bounds supervisor mutations (24/hr). Its reasoning is \"WHY: prevent runaway comments/kills; WHAT: rolling window; HOW CERTAIN: evidence-based; WHAT-NOT-TESTED: starvation of safety actions.\" The cheapest discriminating test for \"breaker is safe\" is \"does a failed-starvation scenario still leave kill budget?\" — and it fails: a single pool is consumed by post_comment AND kickoff_cleanup AND kickoff_stop, so an alert storm (admission failures, UNKNOWN fast-path, doctrine per-dependent posts) can exhaust the 24 budget and leave a genuinely frozen agent unkilled that hour (act_frozen returns FAILED without attempting stop). Dedup reduces storm volume but does not guarantee a safety floor — diverse failure fingerprints refill the pool hourly. This inverts the contract: the safety throttle disarms the safety action it exists to bound.\nDesign implication: finite-state design requires *partitioned authority* — the statechart's terminal transitions (kill/cleanup) must have an explicit, non-starvable contract, while informational transitions (comments/alerts) are throttleable. Fix is a reserved budget (e.g., 20 general + 4 reserved for stop/cleanup) or separate pools; the lifecycle-manager's authority chain (state-owned, CLI-mediated) then has a provable invariant: \"even under maximal alert storm, at least N kill/cleanup actions per hour remain available.\" This is MUST now before unsupervised because unsupervised's defining risk is \"spiral auto-kill either never fires (starved) or fires spuriously (F23+F4 interaction: backup blinds the loop, breaker starves the recovery).\" Closing F4 is the gating item all four reviewers name (\"the gating item before unsupervised operation\" — Pn6g; \"weakest structural gap\" — O8rg; \"release-blocked\" — ccRh).\n\n**Synthesis of the three**: F23 (time), F24 (space), and F4 (permission) are the three axes of the finite-state contract — *when* the supervisor observes, *where* the lifecycle record lives, and *whether* the authority to act remains available. Each violates a different explicit-contract principle, but together they form a single failure mode: a frozen agent that appears during a backup window (F23 blind) on a machine that reboots (F24 lost ledger) while the breaker is exhausted (F4 starved) will never be killed, never be evidenced, and never be retried — the lifecycle silently stalls. The reversed-composition correction is the same for all three: make the statechart's lifecycle ownership explicit and durable, make the engine's scheduling non-blocking and bounded, and make the kill authority's budget non-starvable. None requires architecture rewrite; all are small, bounded fixes that restore the methodology→implementation derivation chain.\n\n---\n§3 — Disposition recommendation summary (operator action list)\n\n**Do before any unsupervised run (MUST now — 4 fixes, all bounded)**:\n1. R10 CWD — `cd \"$REPO_ROOT\"` in bash wrapper after discovery (1 line).\n2. R01 breaker — reserve safety budget / separate pools for stop/cleanup.\n3. R02 phase gating — advance phase only on mutation success; retryable on breaker-block.\n4. R03 LIKELY-FROZEN — 2-cycle counter + `pane_status != alive` gate before kill.\nPlus R18-durability: hot-backup `manager-state.json`/`events.jsonl` off /tmp on every save ( cheapest MUST slice of F24; full dir move preferred).\n\n**Strong value, low cost — do now if touching code (SHOULD now — 7 fixes)**:\n5. R11 slug anchor, 6. R12 chmod 700 + O_NOFOLLOW, 7. R13 schema no-table RED, 8. R14 streamed rclone hash, 9. R15 base-branch config, 10. R16 retry arithmetic, 11. R17 background backup thread, plus R21 DRY_RUN gate fix.\n\n**Keep as-is (DO NOT CHANGE — 9 items)**: R04 parked fix, R05 backup integrity CLOSED, R07 lock, R08 R2 coverage, R22 audit-only naming, R23 DRY_RUN=0, R27 set-e, R28 hygiene bundle, R31 T16 non-hermeticity.\n\n**Defer to v2/v3 (architectural or low-urgency — 11 items)**: R06 crash idempotency checkpointing, R19 JSON CLI, R20 admission prose, R24 catalog empty-cache alert, R25 KEEP/partial/ms-window, R26 rotation/prune caps (beyond R18 SHOULD slice), R29 CYCLE_PROG heredoc migration, R30 scanner identity contract, residual export-remote R05b.\n\n**No code change for #470** — it validates claim-adjudication methodology (26 claims, AGREE on all, doom_loop per-message not per-session, OTel cache_read naming); track separately.\n\n---\n§4 — Cross-review consensus notes\n\n- Completeness vs spec: R1-R4 ✓, F1-F6 ✓ (F4 with caveats now closed), six-verdict matrix fully consumed, wave-anomaly correct. Design-doc items outside dispatch scope (wind-down protocol, provenance-recorded contract check) correctly absent — explicitly defer per dispatch spec, not a gap.\n- Test trajectory 96/98 (T16 CWD) → 181/181 (post-fixpass, in-repo) is consistent and honest; WHAT-NOT-TESTED sections across all four reviewers converge on the same five gaps (non-dry-run mutations, rclone positive path, RED branch, long-duration loops, concurrent instances) — no reviewer claims broader coverage than delivered.\n- Salvage investigation on #466 (O8rg headline \"5 of 6 CLOSED\" vs body 4/1/1) — authoritative reading remains 4 CLOSED /1 PARTIAL (F5) /1 NOT-CLOSED (F4 breaker), as corrected by pp3g-aiRk forensic and confirmed by Pn6g re-verification.\n- All reviewers: CONDITIONAL; land-and-operate defensible for supervised shakedown n=1 with operator watching; NOT for unsupervised until MUST now items closed.\n\n---\nDeliverable: read-only synthesis — no code modified per dispatch.","created_at":"2026-08-26T23:12:55.851739184Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:wV/CDHJyPsmC09K1yKN8pRBOCTvwbZ79hfVzS0pQWJ0","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg73F9p1rLmzPVsqvFb8ZujM+FWnDlf7AO7tQPIi+z4sEAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAqw6xZ9mT4/Hk3pAZAragvMtTUtZ5Cx/hGQXBJtmm0CGZYarnxnbJBW64A+c6FXtVWPLYaeBX2wGXE1DCjrenDQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:wV/CDHJyPsmC09K1yKN8pRBOCTvwbZ79hfVzS0pQWJ0","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLW